q 0 0 0 RG 54.00 680.35 504.00 15.22 re S Q
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 62.00 684.35 Tm (Sixteen operators and analysts agree on one thesis: the permit is the asset, not the steel, and occupancy decides) Tj ET
q 0 0 0 RG 54.00 665.12 504.00 15.22 re S Q
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 62.00 669.12 Tm (whether it pays. The 1965 Highway Beautification Act, local moratoriums, and four state bans freeze supply, so) Tj ET
q 0 0 0 RG 54.00 649.90 504.00 15.22 re S Q
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 62.00 653.90 Tm (buying an existing permitted structure beats building a new one. Ground rent takes 15 to 25% of revenue. Static) Tj ET
q 0 0 0 RG 54.00 634.67 504.00 15.22 re S Q
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 62.00 638.67 Tm (boards clear thin margins that leverage can erase. Operators underwrite at 85% occupancy rather than at rate card.) Tj ET
q 0 0 0 RG 54.00 619.45 504.00 15.22 re S Q
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 62.00 623.45 Tm (Digital conversion turns a static face earning roughly $3,000 a month into $15,000 to $18,000 a month for about) Tj ET
q 0 0 0 RG 54.00 604.22 504.00 15.22 re S Q
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 62.00 608.22 Tm ($200,000, and ledgerandland models net income nearly tripling. The practitioners split on entry cost, on exit) Tj ET
q 0 0 0 RG 54.00 589.00 504.00 15.22 re S Q
BT /F1 10.5 Tf 1 0 0 1 62.00 593.00 Tm (multiples \(3-5x versus 7-12x revenue\), and on market size \($9.46B versus $57.8B\). They also split on how much) Tj ET
q 0 0 0 RG 54.00 573.77 504.00 15.22 re S Q
